{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 16, :comments ["<b>And YHWH Elohim commanded the human, saying: Of every tree of the garden you may freely eat.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): here the human was admonished with two commandments — a positive commandment and a negative commandment. The positive commandment: 'of every tree of the garden you may freely eat.' The negative commandment: 'and of the tree of knowledge of good and evil, you shall not eat of it.'" "<b>And by way of</b> the Midrash: he was commanded here with seven commandments, and they are the seven commandments that the Rabbis of blessed memory stated in several places — the seven commandments of the children of Noah that they were commanded. And these are they: idolatry (עבודה זרה), blessing (i.e., cursing) the Name, laws (דינין), shedding of blood, uncovering of forbidden relations (עריות), theft, and a limb from a living animal. 'ויצו' — this is idolatry, as it is written (Hosea 5:11): ״כי הואיל הלך אחרי צו״. 'ה׳' — this is blessing (cursing) the Name, as it is written (Leviticus 24:16): ״ונוקב שם ה׳ מות יומת״ — 'אלהים' — these are the laws (דינין), as it is said (Exodus 22:27): ״אלהים לא תקלל״. 'על האדם' — this is shedding of blood, as it is said: ״שופך דם האדם״ and so forth. 'לאמר' — these are forbidden relations (עריות), as it is said: ״לאמר הן ישלח איש את אשתו והלכה מאתו והיתה לאיש אחר״. 'מכל עץ הגן אכל תאכל' — this is theft, meaning: from a certain thing and not from another's. 'ומעץ הדעת טוב ורע לא תאכל ממנו' — this is a limb from a living animal, meaning: from a certain thing you shall not eat part of it. Thus you have the seven commandments of the children of Noah alluded to here."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 2, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:09:16.404105466Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}